  - Net burn = expense burn (158K) − revenue (140K) = $18K/month → runway = 650K / 18K ≈ 36 months.
So please confirm whether “burn rate” is gross or net, and whether the $420K last quarter is recurring revenue you expect to continue.

2) Key revenue/retention metrics & formulas (what to calculate)
- Monthly Recurring Revenue (MRR) = quarterly revenue / 3 (if revenue is steady/recurring).
  - With your number: MRR ≈ $140K.
- Annual Recurring Revenue (ARR) = MRR × 12 (or quarterly × 4).
  - ARR ≈ $140K × 12 = $1.68M (or 420K × 4 = $1.68M).
- Revenue Growth (period over period) = (Current − Prior) / Prior.
  - Need prior-period revenue to compute actual growth.
- Compound Quarterly Growth Rate (CQGR) or CAGR over multiple periods: ((End/Start)^(1/n) − 1).
- Gross Margin on revenue = (Revenue − COGS) / Revenue (need COGS).
- Customer Churn Rate (period) = # customers lost / # customers at period start.
- Revenue (Gross) Churn = revenue lost from churned customers / starting revenue.
- Net Revenue Retention (NRR) = (Starting revenue + expansion − contraction − churn) / Starting revenue.
  - NRR >100% means expansion more than offsets churn (highly desirable).
- Customer Lifetime Value (LTV) ≈ ARPA / churn_rate (for subscription businesses), where ARPA = average revenue per account.
- LTV : CAC ratio — useful if you track acquisition spend.

3) Quick example calculations using your numbers (and a few assumptions)
- MRR = 420K / 3 = $140K.
- ARR = $1.68M.
- If revenue is steady at $140K/month:
  - Net burn = 158K − 140K = $18K/month → runway = 650K / 18K ≈ 36 months.
- If revenue grows next quarter by 5% QoQ:
  - Next quarter revenue = 420K × 1.05 = $441K → next-month MRR ≈ $147K.
  - Net burn = 158K − 147K = $11K/month → runway ≈ 650K / 11K ≈ 59 months.
- If instead revenue declines 10% QoQ:
  - Next quarter revenue = 420K × 0.90 = $378K → MRR = $126K.
  - Net burn = 158K − 126K = $32K/month → runway ≈ 650K / 32K ≈ 20 months.

