Thanks — I can help. First, a key inconsistency you should confirm so the metrics are accurate.

Quick checks from the numbers you gave
- Cash on hand: $650,000
- Burn rate: $158,000 / month
- Revenue last quarter: $420,000 → monthly revenue = $420,000 / 3 = $140,000
- Annualize last quarter → ARR ≈ $420,000 * 4 = $1,680,000
- MRR ≈ $1,680,000 / 12 = $140,000

Runway math and inconsistency
- If the stated burn rate ($158K/month) is the net cash burn (expenses minus revenue), runway = cash / net burn = 650,000 / 158,000 ≈ 4.1 months.
- If $158K/month is gross expense and revenue is $140K/month, net burn = 158K − 140K = $18K/month → runway = 650,000 / 18,000 ≈ 36.1 months.
- If your runway truly is 18 months, implied net burn = 650,000 / 18 ≈ $36.1K/month. Given revenue = $140K/month, that would imply gross expenses ≈ $176.1K/month — which does not match the $158K figure you provided.

Please confirm which interpretation is correct:
- Is $158K the net burn (cash outflow after revenue) or the gross monthly expense? 
- Is the 18 months runway an estimate you have from a different calculation (e.g., projected revenue growth or cost cuts)?

Key revenue-growth and retention metrics to compute (with formulas and what you’ll need)
- ARR = last 12 months revenue or annualized quarter = 4 * Q revenue (we computed $1.68M).
- MRR = ARR / 12 (we computed $140K).
- Quarter-over-quarter growth rate = (ThisQuarterRevenue − LastQuarterRevenue) / LastQuarterRevenue. (Need prior quarter revenue.)
- YoY growth = (ThisQuarterRevenue − SameQuarterLastYear) / SameQuarterLastYear. (Need prior-year quarter.)
- Gross Revenue Retention (GRR) = (Starting period revenue − revenue lost to churn) / Starting period revenue. Shows how much recurring revenue you kept ignoring expansion.
- Net Revenue Retention (NRR) = (Starting revenue + expansion − churn − contraction) / Starting revenue. NRR >100% means expansion more than offsets churn.
- Customer churn rate = customers lost in period / customers at start of period.
- Revenue churn = revenue lost from churn / starting revenue.
- LTV (simplified) ≈ ARPA / churn_rate_monthly * gross_margin. (Need avg revenue per account (ARPA), monthly churn, and gross margin.)
- CAC and LTV:CAC ratio (need sales & marketing spend and new customers acquired).

Benchmarks / targets (general guidance)
- SaaS benchmarks: GRR > 90% good, NRR > 100% excellent. Monthly customer churn < 3% typical target for growth SaaS. LTV:CAC ≈ 3:1 target.
- Consulting/services firms often look at revenue retention by client cohort and contract renewal rates; aim to minimize revenue churn and push expansion with higher-margin retainer work.

Practical next steps and scenarios to reach 18 months runway
- Reconcile your burn definition (net vs gross). That will immediately fix the runway number.
- Scenario modeling (I can build this): show runway outcomes if you
  - reduce expenses by 10–25%,
  - grow revenue at X% quarter-over-quarter,
  - improve NRR via upsells (e.g., from 95% to 105%).
- Example quick scenarios (using current numbers):
  - If net burn = $158K → runway ≈ 4.1 months (urgent cost / revenue action needed).
  - If current expenses are $158K and revenue $140K → net burn $18K → runway ≈ 36 months (check which number is accurate).
  - To get to 18 months with current cash and current revenue, net burn must be ≈ $36.1K / month. So either reduce net burn to that level or increase revenue (or do both).

Finance narrative — short draft you can use or adapt
"Our focus is to extend runway while accelerating predictable recurring revenue. Last quarter we generated $420K (annualized to $1.68M ARR; $140K MRR). Current cash on hand is $650K. We are prioritizing three levers: (1) protect and grow existing accounts via targeted upsells to improve net retention, (2) tighten cost structure to optimize runway, and (3) accelerate new business through higher-conversion channels. Reconciling our burn definition and modeling scenarios (cost reduction vs. revenue growth) will show the fastest path to a sustainable 18‑month runway."

What I can do next (pick any)
- Reconcile the runway: I’ll model exact runways under different assumptions (cost cuts, growth rates).
- Build a one-page metrics dashboard (ARR, MRR, QoQ growth, GRR, NRR, runway, burn multiple).
- Prepare a slide or email-ready finance narrative and talking points for leadership or investors.
- Compute retention and cohort analytics if you provide: prior quarter revenue, customer counts by quarter, average contract value, churn counts, ARPA, gross margin.

Tell me which next step you want and confirm whether $158K is net or gross burn (and whether the 18 months runway includes projected growth/cost actions). I’ll run the scenarios and draft the narrative/slide.

