Thanks — I can help. First, a quick data check, then recommended metrics, formulas you can use, a short suggested finance narrative outline, and next steps I can take for you.

1) Quick data check / inconsistency
- You gave: cash = $650,000; runway = 18 months; burn = $158,000/month; revenue last quarter = $420,000.
- Monthly revenue = 420,000 / 3 = 140,000/month.

These numbers don’t all line up — depending on how you define “burn” they imply very different runways:

- If “burn” = net cash burn (expenses minus revenue) = $158k/month → runway = 650,000 / 158,000 ≈ 4.1 months.
- If “burn” = gross expenses = $158k/month, with revenue 140k/month → net burn = 158k − 140k = 18k/month → runway = 650,000 / 18,000 ≈ 36.1 months.
- If runway = 18 months is correct → implied net burn = 650,000 / 18 ≈ 36.1k/month. With revenue 140k/month that implies expenses ≈ 176.1k/month.

Please confirm which definition of “burn” you’re using (gross expenses or net cash burn) and whether the 18-month runway is a firm number or an estimate. I’ll reconcile the rest once you confirm.

2) Key revenue-growth & retention metrics to include (with formulas)
- Quarterly revenue growth (QoQ): (ThisQuarter − LastQuarter) / LastQuarter
- Annualized run-rate (if appropriate): LastQuarterRevenue × 4
- Monthly Recurring Revenue (MRR) and MRR growth (if you have recurring revenue)
- Gross Revenue Retention (GRR): Revenue from existing customers at end of period, excluding expansion / Revenue from same customers at start
- Net Revenue Retention (NRR): Revenue from same customers at end including expansion / Revenue from same customers at start
- Customer Churn Rate (monthly): Customers lost during month / Customers at start of month
- Revenue Churn Rate: Revenue lost from churned customers / Starting period revenue from those customers
- New MRR / New Revenue, Expansion MRR / Expansion revenue
- ARPA (Average Revenue per Account) = Total recurring revenue / # customers
- CAC = Sales & Marketing spend / # new customers acquired
- LTV (rough) = ARPA × Gross Margin % / Churn Rate (for subscription businesses)
- LTV : CAC ratio and CAC payback period = CAC / (monthly contribution per new customer)

3) Which of these to prioritize (for a consulting firm vs. product company)
- Consulting firms: emphasize quarter-over-quarter revenue growth, revenue per client, repeat-client revenue, project margin, and client retention (repeat rate / revenue retention).
- If you have subscription/retainer clients: report GRR and NRR (NRR >100% is a strong signal).
- Always include cash runway scenarios and sensitivity to revenue and cost changes.

4) Quick example calculations (using only numbers you provided)
- Monthly revenue = $140k
- Scenario A (burn = net 158k): runway ≈ 4.1 months (urgent)
- Scenario B (burn = gross 158k): net burn = 18k → runway ≈ 36.1 months (comfortable)
- If runway = 18 months → implied net burn ≈ 36.1k/month (expenses ≈ 176.1k/month)

5) Suggested finance narrative structure (one page)
- Headline: Current position (cash, runway, last quarter revenue, QoQ trend)
- Key drivers: primary revenue streams, top 3 clients, margin profile, concentration risk
- Growth metrics: QoQ growth, ARR/run-rate, ARPA, and churn/retention headline
- Scenarios: Base / Upside / Downside for 6/12/18 months (show cash and runway under each)
- Key asks / actions: hiring freezes, prioritized sales initiatives, pricing or packaging changes, retention efforts, target KPIs
- Close: clear, prescriptive next steps and what you need from leadership/teams

6) Concrete next steps I can do for you now
- Reconcile numbers: I can compute exact runways and build 3 scenarios if you confirm whether burn is net or gross and provide last two quarters’ revenue (or monthly revenue history).
- Create a one-page finance narrative memo or a 3‑slide deck draft for internal review (I can draft and save as a draft email or put into a slide-outline).
- Calculate retention metrics (GRR/NRR) if you supply cohort revenue (or revenue by customer for two periods) or customer counts (start, churned, new).

