Thanks — I can help. First, a quick flag: the three numbers you gave are not internally consistent. I’ll show the math and then list the specific metrics I can calculate for you and what additional inputs I need. After that I’ll give recommended KPIs and a short finance narrative you can use.

Quick calculations and inconsistency check
- Monthly revenue = $420,000 / 3 = $140,000 per month.
- Runway based on cash and burn:
  - With $650,000 cash and $158,000 monthly burn, runway = 650,000 / 158,000 ≈ 4.11 months.
  - If runway is truly 18 months at $158k/month, required cash = 158,000 × 18 = $2,844,000 (i.e., you would need ~$2.844M cash).
  - If runway 18 months is correct with $650k cash, implied burn must be 650,000 / 18 ≈ $36,111/month.

Implication: either the cash, burn, or runway number is incorrect. Which of the three should I assume is authoritative?

Useful metrics I can compute immediately (if you confirm numbers or supply missing ones)
- Growth metrics
  - Quarter-over-quarter (QoQ) growth = (this quarter revenue − prior quarter revenue) / prior quarter revenue.
  - Year-over-year (YoY) growth = (this quarter revenue − same quarter last year) / same quarter last year.
  - Compound annual growth rate (CAGR) if you provide revenue series.
  - Monthly run rate (MRR extrapolation) or ARR (if revenue is recurring): ARR = last 12 months revenue (LTM) or MRR × 12.
- Retention metrics
  - Logo churn rate (customers lost / starting customers).
  - Revenue churn rate (MRR lost from churn / starting MRR).
  - Gross retention = 1 − revenue churn (ignore expansion).
  - Net Revenue Retention (NRR) = (starting ARR + expansion − churn − contraction) / starting ARR. Target: >100% is ideal.
  - Cohort retention curves (if you have cohort-level data).
- Cash/efficiency metrics
  - Runway (months) = cash / net burn.
  - Burn multiple = net burn over period / net new ARR over same period.
  - Burn to revenue ratio = monthly burn / monthly revenue (here 158k / 140k ≈ 1.13).
  - Cash shortfall to reach target runway = required cash − current cash.

Benchmarks & quick interpretation (based on typical SaaS/consulting norms)
- Burn-to-revenue ratio >1 (you’re burning more than you’re bringing in monthly) is a red flag — here you’re spending ~13% more than monthly revenue.
- NRR >100% is a strong sign of healthy expansion; <100% means you must bring in new logos just to maintain ARR.
- Target gross retention depends on business model; for SaaS, aim for >85–90% annually; churn >5–10% annually is concerning.
- Runway below 6 months typically triggers emergency cost or raise actions; 12–18 months is stronger for executing growth plans.

Suggested KPIs to include in internal materials
- Cash balance, net burn, runway (months)
- Revenue this quarter, QoQ and YoY growth rates
- MRR / ARR, LTM revenue
- Gross margin
- Number of customers, ARPA (avg. revenue per account)
- Logo churn (monthly & annualized), revenue churn, gross retention
- Net Revenue Retention (NRR)
- CAC, payback period, LTV, LTV:CAC
- Sales pipeline and conversion rates
- Top 3 levers to extend runway / grow revenue (and projected impact)

Short finance narrative (1–2 paragraphs you can use)
- Current state: “As of today, Northgate Consulting reported revenue of $420K last quarter (≈$140K/month). We have $650K in cash and are burning $158K/month, which gives us approximately 4.1 months of runway at current levels. Our burn-to-revenue ratio is ~1.13, meaning we are spending slightly more than we generate in revenue each month.”
- Key priorities and plan: “Over the next 90 days we will pursue three parallel levers to strengthen our position: 1) accelerate topline through targeted upsell and annual-contract conversions to improve cash collection and NRR; 2) reduce discretionary spend and hiring to lower monthly burn toward a 6–12 month runway target; 3) pursue a bridge financing option if we cannot achieve sufficient cost savings or revenue acceleration quickly. Our goal is to reach a baseline of 12–18 months runway while improving NRR above 100% and reducing monthly churn.”

Next steps — what I need from you to produce exact metrics and slides
- Confirm which of the three is correct if one is wrong (cash, burn, or runway), or confirm they are as stated and I’ll flag the inconsistency in your materials.
- Prior quarter revenue and same quarter last year revenue (for QoQ and YoY growth).
- Whether revenue is recurring (MRR/ARR) or project-based. If recurring, provide MRR or ARR.
- Number of customers, new logos added last quarter, lost customers last quarter.
- Expansion revenue (upsells) and contraction revenue last quarter.
- CAC and average contract length if you track them.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a one-page finance summary for leadership (numbers + narrative + 90-day plan).
- Prepare 3–5 slides for an internal review (current state, KPIs, scenarios, asks).
- Calculate all the metrics above once you provide the missing inputs.

Which do you want me to do next, and can you confirm or correct the cash/burn/runway trio and give prior-quarter revenue and churn/ARR details if available?

