First, a quick data-check — there’s an inconsistency I need you to confirm before I build numbers and a narrative:

- You said: cash = $650,000; burn = $158,000/month; runway = 18 months; revenue last quarter = $420,000.
- Monthly revenue (last quarter) = 420,000 / 3 = 140,000/month.

Those figures imply different runways depending on how you define “burn rate.” Two common interpretations:

1) Burn = net cash burn (i.e., expenses minus revenue) = $158K/month
   - Runway = cash / net burn = 650,000 / 158,000 ≈ 4.11 months

2) Burn = gross cash burn (operating cash outflows) = $158K/month, and revenue is separate
   - Net burn = gross burn − revenue = 158,000 − 140,000 = 18,000/month
   - Runway = cash / net burn = 650,000 / 18,000 ≈ 36.1 months

Neither matches 18 months exactly, so either:
- Your runway assumes future changes (cost cuts, revenue growth, committed financing), or
- One of the inputs (cash, burn, revenue) is misstated or uses different definitions.

Please confirm which of these is true (and whether “burn” is gross or net), or provide the prior-quarter revenue or operating expense detail if available. Meanwhile, below are the concrete metrics, calculations, and a ready-to-use finance narrative you can adapt.

Key calculated metrics (based on the numbers you gave)
- Monthly revenue (last quarter average): 420,000 / 3 = 140,000/month
- If burn is net cash burn: runway ≈ 4.1 months
- If burn is gross expense: net burn = 18,000/month → runway ≈ 36.1 months
- Monthly net cash change (revenue − gross burn) = 140,000 − 158,000 = −18,000/month (cash decreasing)

Recommended revenue-growth metrics to compute and track
- QoQ growth (quarter-over-quarter): (Revenue_this_quarter − Revenue_prev_quarter) / Revenue_prev_quarter
- YoY growth (year-over-year): (Revenue_this_quarter − Revenue_same_quarter_last_year) / Revenue_same_quarter_last_year
- CMGR (compound monthly growth rate): (Revenue_end / Revenue_start)^(1/months) − 1
- MRR (if you have recurring revenue): total MRR and YoY/ QoQ MRR change
- New logo ARR vs expansion ARR vs churned ARR (for SaaS-like businesses)
- Conversion funnel metrics: leads → opportunities → closed ARR

Recommended retention metrics (essential if recurring/repeat revenue)
- Gross Revenue Retention (GRR) = (Starting ARR − Churned ARR) / Starting ARR
- Net Revenue Retention (NRR) = (Starting ARR − Churned ARR + Expansion ARR) / Starting ARR
  - NRR > 100% means expansion more than replaces churn — key lever for growth
- Customer churn rate (by count) = customers lost / customers at start of period
- Revenue churn rate = ARR lost to churn / starting ARR
- Cohort retention curves: % revenue retained month-by-month from cohorts
- LTV (lifetime value) and CAC and payback period

Examples and quick sensitivity demos you can use in slides
- Small changes in retention compound quickly:
  - Example: Starting ARR = $1,000,000
    - If monthly churn = 2% → annual retention roughly (1−0.02)^12 ≈ 78% (so LTM shrink unless expansion)
    - If you reduce monthly churn to 1% → annual retention ≈ 89% — a big lift to NRR and future ARR
- Runway sensitivity: show 3 scenarios (base, best, worst)
  - Base: current revenue stable, gross burn = 158K → net burn = 18K → runway = 36 months
  - Worst: burn is net 158K → runway ≈ 4.1 months
  - Best: revenue grows 10% QoQ for two quarters (MRR +21%) and/or cost reductions of 15% → recalculate net burn and runway

What I recommend you present internally (KPIs + narrative)
- Top-line KPIs: quarterly revenue, QoQ%, YoY%, MRR/ARR (if applicable)
- Retention KPIs: churn %, GRR, NRR, cohort 3/6/12-month retention
- Unit economics: CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, gross margin
- Cash & runway: current cash, net burn, runway under 3 scenarios (base/worst/best)
- Action plan: immediate 90-day actions (revenue acceleration and cost control), 6–12 month strategy (improve retention, upsell strategy, pricing, product improvements), funding plan if runway < 12 months

