Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. First, one important thing to check: the numbers you gave are inconsistent as-is. Before I compute and draft a narrative, please confirm which of these is correct (or tell me how you define “burn rate”):

- Cash: $650K (you gave)
- Burn rate: $158K/month (do you mean gross cash burn = total cash outflows, or net burn = outflows minus revenue?)
- Runway: 18 months (this conflicts with the two numbers above)

Quick reconciliations so you see the mismatch:
- Monthly revenue = $420K ÷ 3 = $140K. Annualized revenue ≈ $1.68M.
- If burn rate = 158K is net burn (already revenue taken into account), runway = 650 / 158 ≈ 4.1 months.
- If burn rate = 158K is gross expenses and revenue is 140K, net burn = 158 − 140 = $18K/month → runway = 650 / 18 ≈ 36.1 months.
- If runway is truly 18 months with $650K cash, implied net burn = 650 / 18 ≈ $36.1K/month (which implies gross expenses ≈ 140 + 36.1 = $176.1K/month).

Tell me which interpretation is correct (or supply the true monthly expenses and whether “burn” is net or gross), and I’ll proceed. Meanwhile, here’s a clear, actionable plan and the metrics I can prepare for you.

What I can calculate and include in the finance materials
- Revenue growth metrics
  - Quarter-over-quarter (QoQ) growth = (ThisQuarter − PrevQuarter) / PrevQuarter
  - Year-over-year (YoY) growth if prior year quarter is available
  - Monthly / quarterly revenue run-rate and CAGR if you provide prior periods
  - Annualized Revenue = last quarter × 4 = $1.68M (from $420K last quarter)
  - Unit economics: ARPA / ARPU, CAC, CAC payback (if you provide CAC and customer counts)

- Retention metrics (required inputs I’ll need listed below)
  - MRR/ARR churn rate = churned MRR during period ÷ MRR at start of period
  - Gross Revenue Retention (GRR) = (MRR_start − churn) / MRR_start
  - Net Revenue Retention (NRR) = (MRR_start + expansion − contraction − churn) / MRR_start
  - Logo churn = customers lost ÷ customers at start
  - Cohort retention curves (e.g., 1, 3, 6, 12-month retention) if you have cohort data
  - LTV and LTV:CAC (if you give CAC, gross margin, ARPA, churn)

Benchmarks (general guidance)
- NRR: >100% is good; >110–120% strong for growth-stage subscription businesses.
- GRR: >90% is healthy for SaaS; >95% is excellent.
- Monthly MRR churn: <1% is a common target for mature SaaS; up to 3% may be acceptable for early-stage or higher-churn markets.
- CAC payback: <12 months preferred for faster scaling; 12–24 months okay depending on runway and margins.

Suggested finance narrative structure (short, punchy, investor/internal friendly)
1. Executive snapshot (1 slide)
   - Cash, runway (explicit how calculated), LQA revenue (last quarter annualized), net burn, ARR run-rate.
2. Growth trajectory (1–2 slides)
   - QoQ / YoY growth charts, CAC trends, ARPA, pipeline conversion rates.
3. Retention & unit economics (1–2 slides)
   - NRR, GRR, logo churn, cohort retention chart, LTV:CAC.
4. Scenario planning & runway sensitivity (1 slide)
   - Base / downside / upside scenarios showing how revenue growth or cost saves extend runway. Example: 10% revenue growth vs 20% cost reduction.
5. Key levers & asks (1 slide)
   - Revenue levers: pricing, upsell/expansion, new channels.
   - Cost levers: hiring freeze, vendor renegotiations, discretionary spend cuts.
   - Fundraising plan if runway < 12 months.
6. Appendix: assumptions & methodology.

Concrete next steps / what I need from you to proceed
- Confirm which burn definition is correct (gross vs net) or provide monthly operating expenses.
- Previous period revenue (prior quarter and preferably prior four quarters) to compute growth rates.
- Customer counts at period start and end, churned customers, new customers (for logo churn).
- MRR by cohort or monthly MRR time series, plus expansion/contraction MRR (for NRR, cohort curves).
- CAC, gross margin, ARPA (for LTV, unit economics).
- Audience and length: internal leadership, board, or investors? (tone differs)

If you want, I can:
- Recalculate runway and provide three scenarios immediately if you confirm burn definition.
- Compute NRR/GRR and churn if you paste the MRR/customer numbers.
- Draft the one-page finance narrative and 6–8 bullet-slide text once data is confirmed.

Which of the above would you like me to do first? If it’s easiest, confirm whether “burn = net cash burn” or paste monthly operating expenses and I’ll run the numbers and draft the narrative.

